# Typing contract for ActiveSupport's temporal intrinsics — the
# `runtime/ruby/db.rbs` pattern: a declaration with NO paired `.rb`
# here, because the implementation ships PER TREE.
#
# Two implementations satisfy this signature. The spinel-subset
# `runtime/spinel/active_support_time_parsing.rb` avoids everything
# spinel's Time lacks (no `require "time"`, no `Time.parse`, no `usec`);
# the CRuby/JRuby overlay's sibling shadows it (dedupe last-wins) with
# the stdlib-backed exact version. Neither lives under `runtime/ruby/`,
# so without this file the framework runtime has no signature for them
# and a call site types as an unresolved variable.
#
# `parse_db_time` hydrates the sqlite3 adapter's bare
# "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS[.ffffff]" TEXT (implicitly UTC) and presents it
# in the app's `config.time_zone`. `json_time` is its JSON-rendering
# sibling: Rails serializes a temporal attribute through
# `TimeWithZone#as_json` = `xmlschema(3)`, not as the stored text.
# `format_db_time` is the write-side inverse.
#
# `rfc2822` is the RSS-feed formatter: `Time#rfc2822` is stdlib `time`
# (NOT core Ruby — `require "time"` installs it), so a `<time>.rfc2822`
# call site can only ground to a module function, the same posture
# `lower::duration` takes for `Integer#days`. See
# `emit::ruby::library::apply_time_format_lowering` for the rewrite.
#
# All of them are nil-safe: an absent column reads as nil / "" and the
# synthesized accessors call them unguarded.
module ActiveSupport
  def self.parse_db_time: (String? str) -> Time?

  def self.json_time: (String? str) -> String?

  def self.format_db_time: (untyped value) -> String?

  def self.db_now: () -> String

  def self.rfc2822: (Time? t) -> String?
end
